# dash-openlayers — AI Agent Rules

`dash-openlayers` bridges a declarative Python/Dash API with an imperative
OpenLayers (Canvas/WebGL) runtime through React. Violating the invariants
below produces code that looks correct but leaks memory, breaks the Python
wrapper generator, or silently mis-projects coordinates. Follow them exactly.

## 1. React & OpenLayers Lifecycle Rules

- Every OpenLayers object (`ol/Map`, `ol/layer/*`, `ol/source/*`,
  `ol/interaction/*`, event listeners via `.on(...)`) MUST be instantiated
  inside a `useEffect`/`useLayoutEffect` hook — never at module scope or
  directly in the render body.
- Every `useEffect` that creates an OpenLayers object MUST return a cleanup
  function that disposes it: `map.removeLayer(layer)`,
  `map.removeInteraction(interaction)`, `map.setTarget(null)`,
  `source.clear()`, `unByKey(listenerKey)`. Do not rely on garbage collection.
- Do not hold OpenLayers instances (`Map`, `layer`, `source`) in React state
  unless the component render output depends on them. Prefer `useRef` for
  imperative handles; use `useState` only for the value(s) consumed by JSX
  (e.g. `OLContext.Provider value={map}` needs state so children re-render
  once the map exists).
- Never mutate an OpenLayers instance stored in a ref during render. All
  mutation happens in effects or event handlers.
- New components consume the map via `useMap()` from
  `src/lib/context/OLContext.js` — never re-implement context lookup or pass
  the map instance through props.

## 2. Dash State Bridge Rules

- Outward event propagation to Python is **only** done via
  `setProps({ propName: value })`. Never invent a new callback mechanism.
- `setProps` is optional at runtime (Dash omits it outside a live app); always
  guard with `if (setProps) { ... }` before calling it.
- Props that Dash can set from Python and that the component also updates
  itself (e.g. `center`, `zoom`) must be documented as bidirectional in their
  JSDoc (see Docstring Invariant below).
- Do not hallucinate React state for data that Python owns. If a prop comes
  from Dash, treat it as the source of truth and call the matching OpenLayers
  setter (`view.setCenter()`, `view.setZoom()`) — do not shadow it in local
  `useState` unless reconciling controlled/uncontrolled updates.

## 3. Docstring Invariant (mandatory)

`npm run build` uses `react-docgen` to turn `propTypes` into the Python
wrapper's type hints and docstrings. A missing JSDoc block means the
generated Python class ships with an empty prop description.

- Every single key in `propTypes` MUST have a JSDoc block comment (`/** ... */`)
  immediately above it describing the prop, its units/coordinate order where
  relevant, and whether it is bidirectional.
- Banned: inline `//` comments on `propTypes` entries — they are not parsed
  by `react-docgen` and do not satisfy the invariant.

  ```javascript
  // DO
  PropTypes.shape({
    /** Component ID, used for Dash callback matching. */
    id: PropTypes.string,
    /** [lon, lat] in EPSG:4326, converted internally per `projection`. */
    center: PropTypes.arrayOf(PropTypes.number),
  });

  // DO NOT
  PropTypes.shape({
    id: PropTypes.string, // component id
  });
  ```

## 4. Styling & Bundle Rules

- Import `ol/ol.css` only at component boundaries that render a map
  (currently `Map.react.js`). Do not re-import it from every component —
  Webpack/Rollup's `css-loader`/`style-loader` will otherwise emit duplicate
  CSS into the bundle.
- Keep OpenLayers/proj4 imports scoped to the file that needs them
  (tree-shaking friendly); do not add a barrel file that re-exports all of
  `ol/*`.

## 5. Coordinate & Projection Rules

- OpenLayers coordinates are always `[x, y]` — i.e. `[lon, lat]` in
  EPSG:4326/EPSG:3857, not `[lat, lon]`. Never pass `[lat, lon]` to
  `fromLonLat`, `View.center`, or a geometry constructor.
- Use `ol/proj` helpers (`fromLonLat`, `toLonLat`, `transform`) for all
  conversions; do not hand-roll trigonometric projection math.
- Custom EPSG definitions arrive from Python as `proj4Defs` (`{code, def}`)
  and must be registered with `proj4.defs(code, def)` followed by
  `register(proj4)` from `ol/proj/proj4` **before** a `View` using that
  projection is constructed.

## 6. Repository File Map

| Path | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `src/lib/components/*.react.js` | React components (OpenLayers wrappers) |
| `src/lib/context/OLContext.js` | Map instance context (`useMap()`) |
| `src/index.js` | Public JS export surface — update when adding a component |
| `dash_openlayers/*.esm.js`, `*.umd.js` | Generated JS bundles — never hand-edit, regenerated by `npm run build` (Rollup) |
| `dash_openlayers/metadata.json`, `dash_openlayers/<Component>.py` | **Hand-maintained** (see §8) — `npm run build` does not run react-docgen or a Python generator here; update these manually alongside `propTypes` changes |
| `tests/` | Python tests (`pytest`) |
| `usage*.py` | Manual/demo Dash apps exercising components |

## 7. Build, Test & Verification Commands

```bash
npm install          # JS deps
npm run build        # rollup build: JS bundles only (ESM + UMD) — see §8
npm run lint         # ESLint over src/
npm run format       # Prettier over src/ and root JS/JSON
uv sync              # Python dev deps (pytest, ruff, pre-commit)
uv run pytest -q     # Python tests
uv run ruff check .  # Python lint
uv run ruff format . # Python format
uv run coverage xml  # coverage CLI lives in the uv venv — always prefix with `uv run`, never call `coverage` bare (breaks in CI)
```

Run `npm run build` after any `propTypes`/component change, then manually
update `dash_openlayers/metadata.json` and the component's Python wrapper
class to match (see §8) — verify the diff under `dash_openlayers/` reflects
only the intended prop surface before committing.

## 8. Python Wrapper & Bundle Generation Reality Check

- `npm run build` only runs Rollup. There is no react-docgen or
  `dash`/`component_generator.py` step wired in, so adding or changing a
  component requires hand-updating **three** places in lockstep:
  1. `src/index.js` (export the component)
  2. `dash_openlayers/metadata.json` (add/update the react-docgen-shaped
     entry — displayName, props, JSDoc-derived `description` strings)
  3. `dash_openlayers/<Component>.py` (the Python `Component` subclass —
     prop list, defaults, docstring)
  Forgetting any one of these leaves Python and the client bundle out of
  sync (e.g. `AttributeError: module 'dash_openlayers' has no attribute
  'X'`, or the browser logging `Component X not found in dash_openlayers`).
- `dash_openlayers/__init__.py`'s `_js_dist` must list **only** the UMD
  bundle (`dash_openlayers.umd.js`). Dash serves `relative_package_path`
  assets as plain `<script src=...>` tags — it only adds `type="module"`
  for `asset_path` files ending in `.mjs`. Listing the ESM build
  (`dash_openlayers.esm.js`) in `_js_dist` makes the browser throw
  `Uncaught SyntaxError: Unexpected token 'export'`. The ESM bundle can
  still be built and referenced via `package-info.json`'s `module` field
  for npm/bundler consumers — just keep it out of `_js_dist`.
- `rollup.config.js` must mark `react`, `react-dom`, and `prop-types` as
  `external` (with `output.globals` for the UMD build). Without this the
  UMD bundle embeds a second copy of React, and once it runs alongside
  Dash's own React instance, hooks like `useRef` fail at runtime
  (`Cannot read properties of null (reading 'useRef')`) and nothing renders.

